d on it because it  requires the Monarchs head on a tutu and  of course in the 20th century we've had  a whole wave of revolutions and  revolutionary struggles which have  deployed violence the Russian Revolution  the Chinese Vietnamese Cuban the huge  anti-colonial struggles waged by the  Vietnamese so I'm afraid it's very  difficult studying modern all medieval  history or early modern history to get  away from this idea of violence and I  think we have to detach the violence  used by masses in motion from acts of  individual terror carried out for  whatever reason or suicide terrorism or  or whatever I mean the struggle of  people for their freedom has always  involved violence because the people  they're trying to gain independence from  deploy it as well so the choice is  either to sit down and do nothing or to  hope that peaceful agitation wins today  which it never has with very few  exceptions and and to throw the power  open to those who occupy oppress and  kill people  ad nauseam I mean this goes on today  even as we're sitting here six walls are  being waged in the United in the world  by the United States the most brutal of  which which is hardly mentioned is the  war in the Yemen being waged by Saudi  Arabia and its allies backed by the  United States in Britain so what are the  poor Yemenis to do thanks very much  indeed you may well discuss exactly that  point in just a few minutes but let's  turn first to Steven Pinker Steven we've  heard that case that peaceful engagement  on its own simply hasn't been a useful  way of changing society with the  exception of a very few cases you  spoken out for better angels career very  publicly would you agree with the case  that tarik has made no I wouldn't agree  I guess I start from the premise that  killing people is bad and killing more  people is worse than killing fewer  people so even though I also don't  support terrorism our terrorists have  killed a tiny number of people the worst  terrorist attack in history 9/11 killed  3,000 typical terrorist attack kills a  handful  whereas Wars and revolutions kill people  by the millions and tens of millions and  often it is true tarik listed a number  of violent events in human history we do  not make the argument that these are are  good or justifiable these were history's  disasters now I do believe that there  are arguments that there can be  occasions in which violence is justified  if it is the only way to prevent greater  violence again I'm assuming that that  murdering people is bad you disagree  with that then you can disagree with the  whole argument and that murdering two  million people 4 million people 20  million people is really really bad now  did the events that we just heard result  in the reduction of violence prevention  of killing of even greater numbers which  I suppose could be used as a utilitarian  argument justifying violence the answer  is in virtually all the cases no the  French Revolution was a disaster killed  2 million people  led to the rise of Napoleon from perhaps  the world's first totalitarian fascist  dictator who began wars of conquest that  killed another an additional four  million people led to the restoration of  slavery to the restoration of the  monarchy and a delay of democracy in  France by perhaps a century Russian  Revolution killed several million led to  the Russian civil war which killed  another nine million led to the rise of  Stalin who killed 20 million there's an  old cliche you can't make an omelet  without breaking a few eggs well it  ignores the fact that people aren't eggs  and that generally does not result in an  omelet again the Chinese revolution  perhaps the most disastrous event in  history led to the Great Leap Forward  and the  Cultural Revolution we've killed perhaps  32 to 40 million people altogether  time and again a violent revolution  violent war in addition to the moral  harm of mass murder and again I mean  murder we're talking about millions or  tens of millions of people does not  result in a stable peaceful state that  saves the lives of even more quite the  contrary  you know a recent study by Maria Stephan  and Erica Chenoweth actually looked over  the last century at violent and  nonviolent resistant movement resistance  movements to put the Gandhian hypothesis  to a test that it that there are ways of  overcoming tyranny using the all of the  tactics that that Gandhi worked out now  you could of course be cynical about the  Gandhian hypothesis by saying well he  tugged at the heartstrings of of the  British at an opportune moment and he  just got lucky so they decided to count  of the all of the resistance movements  of the 20th century they divided them  into violent ones and nonviolent ones  putting aside the question of which was  more moral that is which murdered fewer  people just asked the question of which  is more effective now it's not the case  that violent resistance movements always  succeed or the non violent ones fail or  vice versa  but if you count them up they found that  nonviolent resistance movements were  three times as effective as violent ones  doesn't mean the vile ones are never  effective but even in terms of sheer  efficacy the nonviolent ones tend to  have a higher success when you say three  times as effective Steven do you mean  they killed one-third as few people no  they three times more often they  resulted in regime change  okay that's an interesting definition  and one that I think we will have to  come back and lead in the discussion of  final observation is that in in a survey  of what leads to stable democracies  inspired in part by the 2003 invasion of  Iraq which I think we all agree was not  a successful measure to to install a  peaceful liberal democracy in that  actually fits into a pattern that  decapitations of a existing tyrannical  regime generally don't result in a  stable democracy well that with one  final observation I'm Canadian so we  actually did achieve independence from  Britain nonviolent way and we took a  little bit longer than the United States  but the American Revolution was a pretty  bloody and and brutal mess as well and  Canada has a result it is today one of  the most stable least violent and most  democratic societies on earth so much so  they're a prominent politician in this  country who want Britain now to become  Canada or something called Canada plus  plus plus  Thank You Stephen Ellie started young  you know a great deal but in theory and  in practice about the way in which  coercion and violence and resistance  have been used in certain very prominent  recent conflicts I wonder if you could  give us your thoughts in the next couple  of minutes so I think when we talk about  whether violence or conflict is  necessary for some sort of lasting  change I think it's important when we  discuss these the framework in which we  discuss them in terms of you know we  don't have the time for that right now  but even to acknowledge you know the  history and the roots of the ideas of  war and essentially you know people mass  murdering each other for you know  whatever purpose or whatever means it  may be and I think from the historical  references that we have access to I  think it's quite I think we can quite  comfortably say that the history and the  roots of war also the history on roots  of patriarchy for more debates talks and  interviews subscribe today to the  Institute's of Arts and ideas at IAI TV  you

